        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "b599832e-57ab-5d99-908b-19a5a5f0cae4",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Events: Second Battle of Guam, First Battle of Guam, Battle of Antietam, Maryland Campaign, Pacific campaign of World War II\n- Places: Guam, Mariana Islands, Sharpsburg, Maryland, Antietam Creek, Agat, Hoffman Farm\n- Organizations: United States, Japan, American forces, Japanese forces\n- Topics: American Civil War, World War II\n\nFacts:\n- In 1941, Japan captured Guam, a U.S. territory in the Mariana Islands, from the United States during the First Battle of Guam in the Pacific campaign of World War II.\n- On September 17, 1862, the Battle of Antietam, also known in the South as the Battle of Sharpsburg, was fought near Sharpsburg, Maryland, and Antietam Creek as part of the Maryland Campaign.\n- From September 17, 1862, through the following month, the farm buildings at Hoffman Farm were used as a hospital during the Battle of Antietam in the American Civil War.\n- From July 21 to August 10, 1944, the Second Battle of Guam was the American recapture of Japanese-held Guam.\n- In 1944, the beaches of Agat were one of the landing sites of American forces in the Battle of Guam, during which American forces retook the island from occupying Japanese forces.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "02ae2b33-bbf9-50a4-a63c-66aaee1fb128",
        "text": "Page: Battle of Guam (1944). Sentence: The Second Battle of Guam (21 July – 10 August 1944) was the American recapture of the Japanese-held island of Guam, a U.S. territory in the Mariana Islands captured by the Japanese from the U.S. in the 1941 First Battle of Guam during the Pacific campaign of World War II.\nPage: Battle of Antietam. Sentence: The Battle of Antietam , also known as the Battle of Sharpsburg, particularly in the South, was fought on September 17, 1862, near Sharpsburg, Maryland and Antietam Creek as part of the Maryland Campaign.\nPage: Agat Invasion Beach. Sentence: The beaches of Agat were one of the landing sites of American forces in the 1944 Battle of Guam, in which the island was retaken from occupying Japanese forces.\nPage: Hoffman Farm. Sentence: The farm buildings were used as a hospital during the American Civil War in Battle of Antietam from the day of the battle on September 17, 1862, and through the following month.",
